Primary Stabbing Headache
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Sudden transient ultra-short jolts of pain, 'Ice-pick' stabbing quality, Irregular and unprovoked frequency, Normal comprehensive neurological examination

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-041
Reason for Visit: Severe, sudden-onset sharp head pains causing acute panic and fear of an intracranial hemorrhage.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing highly frequent, excruciating 'ice-pick' head pains mimicking an acute subarachnoid hemorrhage or sentinel bleed. Urgent after-hours assessment was critically required to conduct an immediate neurological examination, definitively exclude life-threatening structural brain pathology, and de-escalate acute psychological distress prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "It feels like an ice pick is being driven into my eye for a split second, it's terrifying and I drop to my knees."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 48-hour exacerbation of recurrent, extremely sudden, and highly transient severe sharp jolts of pain strictly localized to the head. The individual attacks have an ultra-short duration, lasting merely a fraction of a second. The frequency tonight is highly unpredictable and unprovoked, occurring up to 20 times in the last hour. The pain predominantly localizes to the orbital and temporal regions but rapidly shifts locations between attacks. There is a complete absence of underlying cranial autonomic symptoms (lacrimation, ptosis) or continuous nausea.
Functional Status: Highly agitated, wincing suddenly mid-sentence during attacks, pacing the room.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound anxiety regarding a catastrophic neurological event; living alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, occasionally grimacing and grabbing the head during brief pain spikes.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive neurological examination is completely normal (cranial nerves II-XII intact, power 5/5, normal reflexes, normal coordination). Fundoscopy reveals sharp optic discs with no papilledema. Temporal arteries are non-tender, non-nodular, with bounding pulses. Neck is supple with no meningism (negative Kernig's/Brudzinski's signs). Documented history of a normal structural brain MRI.
Environmental Observations: House is fully lit; patient demonstrates no intense photophobia between attacks.
Medication Reconciliation: Attempted paracetamol and ibuprofen with zero efficacy due to the ultra-brief nature of the pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Primary Stabbing Headache - Status: Acute high-frequency flare
Differentials:

Subarachnoid Hemorrhage - Ruled out by the ultra-short duration of pain (seconds rather than continuous, unremitting severe pain), normal neurological exam, and lack of meningism or vomiting.

Trigeminal Neuralgia - Ruled out clinically as the pain involves the upper cranium and shifts locations unpredictably, rather than strictly following the V2/V3 nerve branches with specific facial trigger zones.

Temporal Arteritis - Ruled out by normal, non-tender temporal arteries, normal vision, and the highly transient, stabbing nature of the pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to a highly distressing cluster of severe, ultra-brief head pains that clinically simulated a potentially lethal intracranial vascular event. The intensity of the pain and the patient's acute panic necessitated an immediate, hands-on neurological examination. By confirming absolutely intact neurology, normal fundoscopy, and ruling out meningism, the physician definitively diagnosed a benign primary headache syndrome. This immediate clinical exclusion of a structural brain emergency safely aborted a highly distressing psychological crisis and prevented an unnecessary emergency department admission, fulfilling the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided immediate, firm clinical reassurance of intact structural neurology. Prescribed a short course of oral Indomethacin 25mg TDS for 3 days, uniquely effective in breaking the cycle of indomethacin-responsive primary headache syndromes.
Positive Behavioral Change: When a stab occurs, consciously drop the shoulders and exhale slowly, rather than tensing the neck and holding the breath in anticipation of the next attack. This physical relaxation actively prevents secondary tension headaches from developing on top of the primary stabs, directly rewarding the patient with a lower overall baseline pain level and increased autonomic control.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to assess the response to Indomethacin.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if developing a sudden, severe, continuous 'thunderclap' headache that does not stop, sudden weakness in the face or limbs, sudden loss of vision, or severe neck stiffness with a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on normal clinical neurology today; if any new, continuous symptoms or focal weakness develop, immediate reassessment is required.
Clinical Course: Primary stabbing headache is a benign 'misfiring' of the sensory nerves in the scalp. It is incredibly painful but causes absolutely no brain damage. The attacks usually occur in unpredictable clusters and respond specifically to targeted anti-inflammatories.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if the pain suddenly becomes continuous and extremely severe, if your face droops, or if you lose your vision.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your brain and blood vessels are completely fine. Standard painkillers like paracetamol fail because the stab is over before the pill can even enter your bloodstream. Indomethacin is prescribed because it specifically alters the baseline chemical threshold of these hyperactive nerves to prevent the stabs from firing in the first place.
Actionable Steps: Take the Indomethacin strictly with food to protect your stomach. Documenting the frequency of the stabs in a notebook will explicitly prove to you that the medication is working, reinforcing adherence to the short course and actively lowering your severe health anxiety.

